# CAGE Software Delivery Lifecycle — Agent Rule Set
# Authority: docs/GIT_WORKFLOW_STANDARDS.md, docs/DEPLOYMENT_RULES.md,
#            docs/V2_RELEASE_PLAN.md, docs/CHANGE_MANAGEMENT_PROCESS.md,
#            .github/pull_request_template.md, .github/workflows/ci.yml
# These rules are NON-NEGOTIABLE. They are always active, always evaluated,
# and always applied regardless of the nature of the task being performed.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 1 — COMMIT MESSAGE RULES (Conventional Commits v1.0)
# Source: docs/GIT_WORKFLOW_STANDARDS.md §2
# ============================================================

# 1.1 Every commit message the agent writes or suggests MUST follow:
#     <type>(<scope>): <short summary>
#     where the entire subject line is ≤ 72 characters.

# 1.2 VALID TYPES — only these ten are permitted:
#     feat | fix | docs | style | refactor | perf | test | chore | ci | revert
#     No other type is acceptable. If a change does not fit, decompose it.

# 1.3 VALID SCOPES (optional but strongly recommended — use exactly one):
#     gateway | compliance | infra | governance | tests | docs | ci |
#     agentsight | advisor | nemo | opa
#     Omit parentheses entirely when no scope applies.

# 1.4 SUBJECT LINE RULES:
#     - Imperative mood: "add rate limiter" NOT "added" or "adds"
#     - No period at end
#     - No capitalisation of first word of description
#     - Be specific: convey WHAT changed, not just that something changed
#     - PROHIBITED subjects (hook will reject): "fix stuff", "update", "WIP",
#       "fixup!", "squash!", "done", "misc changes", "addressing review comments"

# 1.5 BODY (optional, required when subject is not self-evident):
#     - Separate from subject with exactly one blank line
#     - Wrap every line at 72 characters
#     - Explain WHAT changed and WHY — not how (the diff shows how)
#     - Reference design decisions, trade-offs, ADRs, or relevant context

# 1.6 FOOTER TOKENS:
#     Issue refs:       Closes #<n>  |  Refs #<n>
#     Breaking change:  BREAKING CHANGE: <description>
#     Co-authorship:    Co-authored-by: Name <email>
#     Breaking changes may also use the ! shorthand: feat(gateway)!: ...

# 1.7 REVERT commits must reference the reverted SHA in the body.

# 1.8 MEGA-COMMITS are prohibited. A single commit touching > ~20 files or
#     > ~500 lines (excluding generated files) must be decomposed.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 2 — BRANCH NAMING RULES
# Source: docs/GIT_WORKFLOW_STANDARDS.md §3
# ============================================================

# 2.1 ALL branch names must use lowercase kebab-case.
#     No underscores. No uppercase. No spaces.

# 2.2 REQUIRED PATTERNS:
#     feat/<ticket-id>-short-description   (new feature — ticket ID mandatory)
#     fix/<ticket-id>-short-description    (bug fix — ticket ID mandatory)
#     chore/<description>                  (tooling, deps, build)
#     docs/<description>                   (documentation only)
#     release/v<X.Y.Z>                     (release candidate branch)
#     hotfix/<ticket-id>-description       (production hotfix)
#     refactor/<description>               (code restructuring)
#     ci/<description>                     (CI/CD pipeline changes)
#     spike/<description>                  (experiment / proof-of-concept)

# 2.3 The description segment after the prefix must be ≤ 30 characters.

# 2.4 Feature and fix branches are SHORT-LIVED. Delete after PR merge.
#     Never leave stale branches on the remote.

# 2.5 PROTECTED BRANCHES — direct push is ALWAYS prohibited:
#     main | rc-v2.0.0 | release/**
#     The pre-push hook enforces this locally. GitHub branch protection
#     enforces it server-side. No exceptions, including for maintainers.

# 2.6 ALWAYS branch from the latest integration branch:
#     git checkout main && git pull origin main before branching.
#     Never branch from a stale local copy.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 3 — PULL REQUEST STANDARDS
# Source: docs/GIT_WORKFLOW_STANDARDS.md §4,
#         .github/pull_request_template.md
# ============================================================

# 3.1 PR TITLE must follow Conventional Commits exactly:
#     type(scope): description
#     The PR title becomes the squash-merge commit on the integration branch.
#     A malformed PR title produces a malformed commit on main.

# 3.2 ALL sections of .github/pull_request_template.md must be completed:
#     - Summary: one paragraph (what + why, not a file list)
#     - Type of Change: check every applicable box
#     - Related Issues / ADRs: Closes #n or explicit N/A with justification
#     - Changes Made: bullet list of specific files/components and reasons
#     - Testing: check every applicable method; explain if no tests needed
#     - Compliance & Security Checklist: all items checked
#     - Deployment Notes: migration steps, env var changes, or N/A

# 3.3 CI REQUIREMENTS — all checks must be green before merge:
#     - License Guard (.github/workflows/license_guard.yml)
#     - CI suite (.github/workflows/ci.yml): pytest, STPA freshness,
#       Langfuse posture dry-run, license headers
#     - Security scan (.github/workflows/security-scan.yml)
#     A single failing check blocks merge. Never ask reviewers to approve
#     a PR with failing CI.

# 3.4 REVIEW REQUIREMENTS:
#     - At least one approving review from a maintainer before merge
#     - Author may not approve their own PR
#     - All blocking review comments must be resolved before merge

# 3.5 MERGE STRATEGY:
#     - Squash merge for all feat/fix/chore/docs/ci/refactor PRs
#     - Merge commits reserved for merging rc-v* into main at release time
#     - Rebase merging is NOT used in this repository

# 3.6 COMPLIANCE & SECURITY CHECKLIST (universal — all PRs):
#     - No secrets, credentials, or PII in committed files
#     - Network policy unchanged OR reviewed by security owner
#     - OPA policy changes reviewed for correctness
#     - Data residency: new storage paths guarded by CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION
#     - ISO 42001 / CSA AARM: lula validate passes for all lula-validation-*.yaml
#     - No region-specific behaviour without a cage_deployment_region guard

# 3.7 SHARED-MODULE IMPACT: Any PR touching src/gateway/governance/,
#     src/compliance_bridge/, config/compliance/, config/thresholds/, or
#     config/oscal/ must declare cross-region impact for US_FED, EU_ECB,
#     and APAC_MAS in the PR description.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 4 — VERSIONING & RELEASE STRATEGY
# Source: docs/V2_RELEASE_PLAN.md, docs/V2_RELEASE_RUNBOOK.md
# ============================================================

# 4.1 SEMANTIC VERSIONING: All releases follow SemVer (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH).
#     - MAJOR: breaking changes to public API or authorization boundary
#     - MINOR: new features, backward-compatible
#     - PATCH: bug fixes, backward-compatible

# 4.2 PRE-RELEASE LABELS:
#     v<X.Y.Z>-dev.<N>   — development snapshot (not for production)
#     v<X.Y.Z>-rc.<N>    — release candidate (on rc-v<X.Y.Z> branch)
#     v<X.Y.Z>           — stable release (tagged on rc-v<X.Y.Z> after all gates pass)

# 4.3 RELEASE BRANCH LIFECYCLE:
#     1. Branch rc-v<X.Y.Z> from main when feature freeze begins
#     2. All P0/P1 blockers resolved via PRs targeting rc-v<X.Y.Z>
#     3. Stable tag v<X.Y.Z> applied to rc-v<X.Y.Z> HEAD after all gates pass
#     4. rc-v<X.Y.Z> merged into main via merge commit (not squash)
#     5. rc-v<X.Y.Z> branch retained as a permanent release boundary marker

# 4.4 TAGGING PROCEDURE — only after ALL release gates are green:
#     git tag -a v<X.Y.Z> -m "chore(release): stable v<X.Y.Z>\n\n<summary>"
#     git push origin v<X.Y.Z>
#     Tags must be annotated (not lightweight). Message must follow
#     Conventional Commits format with type "chore" and scope "release".

# 4.5 GITHUB RELEASE: Created via `gh release create` or GitHub UI after
#     tagging. Must reference CHANGELOG.md for release notes. Must be
#     marked as "Latest release" for stable releases.

# 4.6 FEATURE FREEZE: Once rc-v<X.Y.Z> is branched, no new features may
#     be merged into it. Only P0/P1 bug fixes and compliance remediations
#     are permitted on the release branch.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 5 — RELEASE GATE REQUIREMENTS
# Source: docs/V2_RELEASE_PLAN.md §8
# ============================================================

# 5.1 UNIVERSAL GATES (all regions — must pass for ANY stable release):
#     - All ISO 42001 Lula assertions pass (lula-validation-a52.yaml,
#       lula-validation-a53.yaml, lula-validation-a92.yaml)
#     - CSA AARM Lula assertion passes (lula-validation-aarm-vectors.yaml)
#     - SBOM generated and validated
#     - Container image vulnerability scan passes (Trivy — no CRITICAL unmitigated)
#     - Secret detection scan passes (zero secrets in codebase)
#     - All unit and integration tests pass (pytest — 0 failures)
#     - STPA freshness check passes (scripts/check_stpa_freshness.py)
#     - Langfuse posture verified (scripts/verify_langfuse_posture.py)
#     - git log --all -S "<any-credential>" returns zero matches
#     - governed-financial-advisor READY 1/1, AVAILABLE 1
#     - CAGE_ROUTING_SEAL_SECRET present in advisor-secrets (>=64 chars)
#     - GOVERNANCE_SALT present in advisor-secrets (>=64 chars)
#     - Unsigned gateway request returns 403
#     - Valid signed gateway request returns 200
#     - security-scanner-cronjob exists in governance-stack namespace
#     - PSA labels applied: governance-stack=restricted, langfuse=baseline, vllm=baseline
#     - git tag v<X.Y.Z> pushed to origin
#     - GitHub Release published as Latest
#     - terraform.auto.tfvars confirmed gitignored (no secrets in working tree)

# 5.2 US_FED GATES (CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION=US_FED only):
#     - All 10 NIST SP 800-53 Lula assertions pass
#     - NIST SP 800-53 coverage >=45% (via oscal_ssp_exporter.py)
#     - ATO process initiated (OSCAL SSP submitted to AO)
#     - POAM items documented for all open findings

# 5.3 EU_ECB GATES (CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION=EU_ECB only):
#     - EU AI Act compliance posture verified
#     - GDPR data residency confirmed: all paths within europe-west1
#     - DORA Art. 10 audit logging enabled in eu-dev.tfvars
#     - SR 26-2 telemetry suppression active ("no legal force" sentinel intact)

# 5.4 APAC_MAS GATES (CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION=APAC_MAS only):
#     - MAS FEAT compliance posture verified
#     - MAS TRM §4.2 data residency confirmed: all paths within asia-southeast1
#     - MAS Notice 655 audit logging enabled in apac-dev.tfvars
#     - SR 26-2 telemetry suppression active ("no legal force" sentinel intact)


# ============================================================
# SECTION 6 — DEPLOYMENT RULES
# Source: docs/DEPLOYMENT_RULES.md
# ============================================================

# 6.1 GKE DEPLOYMENTS MUST USE CLOUD BUILD — local Docker builds are
#     prohibited for GKE targets. This is non-negotiable.
#     APPROVED methods:
#       ./deploy_all.sh --target gcp-gke --env <dev|prod>
#       gcloud builds submit --config deployment/docker/cloudbuild_gateway.yaml
#       cage-infrastructure MCP server with target: "gcp-gke"
#     PROHIBITED for GKE:
#       docker build ... && docker push ...
#       docker-compose build && docker-compose push
#       kubectl apply without a preceding Cloud Build

# 6.2 LOCAL / AGNOSTIC targets may use local Docker:
#     ./deploy_all.sh --target agnostic --env dev   (k3d/kind)
#     ./deploy_all.sh                               (docker-compose)

# 6.3 DEPLOYMENT TARGET MATRIX:
#     gcp-gke  + prod  → Cloud Build  → ./deploy_all.sh --target gcp-gke --env prod
#     gcp-gke  + dev   → Cloud Build  → ./deploy_all.sh --target gcp-gke --env dev
#     agnostic + dev   → Local Docker → ./deploy_all.sh --target agnostic --env dev
#     docker-compose   → Local Docker → ./deploy_all.sh

# 6.4 CLOUD BUILD CONFIG FILES:
#     Gateway:  deployment/docker/cloudbuild_gateway.yaml (primary)
#     vLLM:     deployment/docker/cloudbuild.vllm.yaml
#     LULA:     deployment/docker/cloudbuild.lula.yaml

# 6.5 TERRAFORM STATE: Never manually edit Terraform state. Always use
#     terraform plan → terraform apply. State is in GCS backend
#     (infra/targets/gcp-gke/backend.tf). Active IaC is under infra/;
#     deployment/terraform/ is historical reference only.

# 6.6 terraform.auto.tfvars is ALWAYS gitignored. Never commit it.
#     Secret values go in terraform.auto.tfvars only — never in .tf files.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 7 — HOTFIX WORKFLOW
# Source: docs/GIT_WORKFLOW_STANDARDS.md §3, docs/CHANGE_MANAGEMENT_PROCESS.md §7
# ============================================================

# 7.1 Hotfixes branch from the affected release branch (rc-v<X.Y.Z> or main):
#     git checkout rc-v<X.Y.Z> && git pull origin rc-v<X.Y.Z>
#     git checkout -b hotfix/<ticket-id>-description

# 7.2 Hotfix branch naming: hotfix/<ticket-id>-description
#     Description segment must be ≤ 30 characters.

# 7.3 Hotfix PRs target the release branch (rc-v<X.Y.Z>), not main.
#     After merge to rc-v<X.Y.Z>, a follow-on PR cherry-picks to main.

# 7.4 Emergency changes (Cat-E) follow the same branch/PR process but
#     with expedited approval: ISSO + System Owner verbal authorization
#     within 2 hours; AO notification within 1 hour of implementation;
#     full documentation completed within 4 hours post-implementation.

# 7.5 Post-emergency: Full CAB retrospective within 5 business days.
#     Root cause analysis documented. POAM item created if systemic gap found.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 8 — FEATURE FREEZE & ENVIRONMENT PROMOTION
# Source: docs/V2_RELEASE_PLAN.md §4.6, docs/CHANGE_MANAGEMENT_PROCESS.md §2
# ============================================================

# 8.1 FEATURE FREEZE triggers when rc-v<X.Y.Z> is branched from main.
#     After freeze: only P0/P1 bug fixes and compliance remediations
#     may target the release branch. No new features.

# 8.2 ENVIRONMENT PROMOTION ORDER: dev → staging → production.
#     No direct promotion from dev to production.
#     Each promotion requires all CI gates to pass on the target branch.

# 8.3 CHANGE CATEGORIES govern promotion approval lead times:
#     Cat-E (Emergency):  2-hour approval window; immediate implementation
#     Cat-S (Standard):   Pre-approved; no individual CAB approval needed
#     Cat-N (Normal):     5 business days minimum; full CAB review
#     Cat-M (Major):      30 calendar days minimum; CAB + AO review

# 8.4 MAJOR CHANGES (Cat-M) that require AO pre-approval before promotion:
#     - New GCP services or Kubernetes namespaces
#     - New external API integrations
#     - New AI models or inference services
#     - Changes to HIGH-impact NIST SP 800-53 controls
#     - Significant security architecture changes

# 8.5 CHANGELOG.md must be updated for every Cat-N and Cat-M change.
#     Format: [CR-YYYY-NNN] description, reviewed-by, approved date,
#     implemented date, Lula validation result.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 9 — SECRET & CREDENTIAL HYGIENE
# Source: docs/V2_RELEASE_PLAN.md §3.2, docs/GIT_WORKFLOW_STANDARDS.md §6
# ============================================================

# 9.1 NEVER commit secrets, credentials, API keys, passwords, tokens,
#     or PII to any file tracked by git. This includes:
#     - Hardcoded fallback values in source code (e.g., os.environ.get("KEY", "secret"))
#     - Inline values in Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, or Terraform .tf files
#     - Any value matching patterns: pk-lf-*, sk-lf-*, hf_*, GOOG*, redis://*:*@*

# 9.2 terraform.auto.tfvars is ALWAYS gitignored. Secret values belong
#     there only. Never reference secret values in committed .tf files.

# 9.3 Before staging any file, verify no credential strings are present:
#     git diff --cached | grep -iE '(password|secret|token|key)' \
#       | grep -v 'secretKeyRef\|secretRef\|var\.\|variable\|sensitive'

# 9.4 Kubernetes Secrets must use secretKeyRef or secretRef — never
#     hardcoded `value:` fields for sensitive data.

# 9.5 If a secret is accidentally committed, treat it as compromised
#     immediately. Rotate before rewriting history. History rewrite
#     does not revoke live credentials.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 10 — LICENSE HEADER REQUIREMENTS
# Source: .github/workflows/ci.yml (license-check job)
# ============================================================

# 10.1 ALL new source files in src/ must include an Apache 2.0 license
#      header OR a "Copyright 2026" notice. The CI license-check job
#      enforces this for *.py, *.js, *.ts, *.tsx files.

# 10.2 Standard header for Python files:
#      # Copyright 2026 Google LLC
#      #
#      # Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the "License");
#      # you may not use this file except in compliance with the License.
#      # You may obtain a copy of the License at
#      #
#      #     https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
#      #
#      # Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software
#      # distributed under the License is distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS,
#      # WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.
#      # See the License for the specific language governing permissions and
#      # limitations under the License.

# 10.3 When creating any new .py, .ts, .tsx, or .js file under src/,
#      prepend the Apache 2.0 header before any other content.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 11 — COMPLIANCE ARTIFACT OBLIGATIONS
# Source: docs/CHANGE_MANAGEMENT_PROCESS.md §5, §9
# ============================================================

# 11.1 Any change that alters a NIST SP 800-53 control implementation
#      requires an OSCAL component update in compliance/oscal/ within
#      2 business days of the PR merge.

# 11.2 Any change that adds or removes Kubernetes resources referenced
#      by Lula validation files requires a Lula validation update in
#      compliance/lula/ in the same PR or a follow-on PR.

# 11.3 POAM items must be updated when a change remediates an open finding.
#      Update docs/POAM.md with: commit SHA, Lula result, closure date.

# 11.4 STPA artifacts must remain fresh. The CI stpa-freshness-check job
#      runs scripts/check_stpa_freshness.py on every push. If STPA
#      source files change, regenerate the STPA artifacts before committing.

# 11.5 Langfuse posture must be verified via scripts/verify_langfuse_posture.py
#      before any release. The CI langfuse-posture-check job enforces this.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 12 — SHARED-MODULE REGION GUARD OBLIGATIONS
# Source: .github/pull_request_template.md §Compliance & Security Checklist
# ============================================================

# 12.1 This is a shared monorepo. src/gateway/governance/ and
#      src/compliance_bridge/ deploy simultaneously to US_FED, EU_ECB,
#      and APAC_MAS via CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION. A change to any shared
#      module affects all three compliance postures.

# 12.2 Any new storage path, GCS write, Langfuse sink, or telemetry
#      export introduced in shared modules MUST be gated on
#      CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION to prevent silent cross-region data leakage
#      (GDPR Art. 44 / MAS TRM §4.2).

# 12.3 EU_ECB data paths must remain within europe-west1.
#      APAC_MAS data paths must remain within asia-southeast1.
#      US_FED data paths must remain within us-central1 or approved US regions.

# 12.4 The "no legal force" SR 26-2 sentinel in EU and APAC baselines
#      must never be removed. Its presence suppresses telemetry that
#      lacks legal basis under GDPR / MAS Notice 655.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 13 — CHANGE MANAGEMENT INTEGRATION
# Source: docs/CHANGE_MANAGEMENT_PROCESS.md §2, §3, §9
# ============================================================

# 13.1 When generating commit messages for production changes, include
#      the Change Request ID in the body when one exists:
#      Format: [CR-YYYY-NNN] <description>

# 13.2 Cat-M changes (new GCP services, new K8s namespaces, new AI models,
#      new external APIs, HIGH-impact control changes) require AO pre-approval.
#      The agent must flag these and refuse to generate implementation steps
#      without noting the Cat-M requirement.

# 13.3 Cat-E changes follow the same branch/PR process but with expedited
#      approval. The agent must still produce a valid branch name, commit
#      message, and PR description — speed does not waive format requirements.

# 13.4 CHANGELOG.md must be updated for every Cat-N and Cat-M change.
#      When generating PR descriptions or commit bodies for such changes,
#      include a CHANGELOG.md update reminder.

# ============================================================
# SECTION 14 — AGENT ENFORCEMENT SUMMARY
# These are the hard stops the agent applies before any output.
# ============================================================

# 14.1 BEFORE suggesting or writing any commit message:
#      - Verify type is one of the 10 permitted types
#      - Verify subject line ≤ 72 characters
#      - Verify imperative mood, no trailing period, no capitalisation
#      - Reject prohibited subjects outright

# 14.2 BEFORE suggesting any branch name:
#      - Verify pattern matches one of the 9 permitted prefixes
#      - Verify description segment ≤ 30 characters
#      - Verify lowercase kebab-case only

# 14.3 BEFORE suggesting any GKE deployment command:
#      - Verify Cloud Build is used (not local docker build/push)
#      - Reject any suggestion of `docker build` + `docker push` for GKE

# 14.4 BEFORE suggesting any file creation in src/:
#      - Prepend Apache 2.0 license header for .py/.ts/.tsx/.js files
#      - Verify no secrets or credentials are embedded

# 14.5 BEFORE suggesting any Terraform change:
#      - Verify secret values are in terraform.auto.tfvars (gitignored)
#      - Verify no sensitive values appear in committed .tf files
#      - Remind that terraform plan must precede terraform apply

# 14.6 BEFORE suggesting any release tag or version bump:
#      - Verify all universal release gates are listed as prerequisites
#      - Verify tag is annotated (not lightweight)
#      - Verify tag message follows Conventional Commits with chore(release)

# 14.7 BEFORE suggesting any PR description:
#      - Verify all sections of .github/pull_request_template.md are addressed
#      - Verify compliance checklist items are included
#      - Verify shared-module cross-region impact is declared if applicable

# 14.8 BEFORE suggesting any change to shared compliance modules:
#      - Flag CAGE_DEPLOYMENT_REGION guard requirement
#      - Flag cross-region impact declaration requirement
#      - Flag Lula validation re-run requirement post-merge

